  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Ohio has received $6.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 53,645 awards with an average award size of $127K. Obligations fell 30.9% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $4.5B. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest industry by obligations at $1.3B. Other sizable industry categories include Petroleum Refineries at $586M and Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $550M. GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY leads vendors with $620M in obligations, followed by MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP at $530M. Obligations by fiscal year show fluctuation: FY21 $8.4B, FY22 $7.8B, FY23 $10.1B, FY24 $9B, FY25 $9.8B, and FY26 $6.8B. All figures are contract obligations and FY2026 totals are partial because they cover the fiscal year to date. This snapshot highlights current buying patterns, the largest vendor awards, and the industries that capture federal dollars in Ohio during FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive federal spending in Ohio?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $4.5B in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration obligated $512M and the Department of Energy obligated $463M. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $362M and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $256M. Other significant buyers include the Department of Homeland Security with $175M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $114M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries capture the most obligations?",
        "body": "GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY received $620M in FY2026 to date and MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP received $530M. SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC received $346M and BATTELLE MEMORIAL INSTITUTE received $238M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) captured $1.3B and Petroleum Refineries captured $586M."
      }
    ]
  },
